// Benchmark: general--zero-delay-send--positive
// Exercises the general fragment's zero-delay send: the
// constructor's single after(0) self-send schedules one
// instantaneous event at logical time 0, which fires before
// any positive time passes. The capability is bounded by
// construction: after(0) occurs exactly once, and the
// re-arming send inside ping uses a positive delay, so the
// model's instantaneous scheduling is a single event and
// logical time advances by one every period thereafter. The
// positive-delay re-arm exists because the mandatory RMC
// stage requires deadlock-freedom: a model that sends one
// message and then quiesces reaches an empty-queue state,
// which RMC reports as a deadlock. This is the bounded
// spelling of the accepted zero-delay capability, distinct
// from the external corpus screen's Minimal failure, whose
// after(0) recurrence never advances logical time past the
// runtime wall-clock guard. No such instantaneous recurrence
// exists here: only the constructor's single after(0) send.
reactiveclass Pulse(3) {
    statevars {
        int fired;
    }
    Pulse() {
        fired = 0;
        self.ping() after(0);
    }
    msgsrv ping() {
        fired = 1;
        self.ping() after(1);
    }
}

main {
    Pulse pulse():();
}
